  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 500 AM MDT Sun Oct 4 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nDry and mild conditions persist across the Northern Rockies and \nMT through much of the upcoming work week as large scale ridging \nremains centered across the SW US with the ridge flattening some \nafter Monday. The warmest daytime temperatures are on track for \nMonday as increased mid-level westerly flow over the flattening \nridge provides some additional warming to the lee of the Rockies. \nThis also results in increased surface winds on Monday, \nparticularly across north-central MT where gusts in excess of 30 \nmph are likely in areas adjacent to the Rockies and central MT \nmountains. After peaking around 20 degrees above seasonal averages\nMonday with a few locations potentially approaching daily \nrecords, temperatures are likely to remain 10-15 degrees above \naverage through Friday. \n\nA significant change in the weather is appearing more likely next \nweekend with increasing support from longer range model ensembles \nfor upper level troughing across the NW US and at least a modest drop \nin temperatures and increased risk for precipitation. The shift in the \npattern is the downstream result of complicated interactions that \noccur across northwest Pacific this week between a Typhoon and cold \nupper level system moving out of NE Asia. This contributes to \nconsiderable uncertainty and likely fluctuations in the forecast \nwith more details to come as the week progresses.  \n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nThe main weather hazard to watch over the coming days will be \nelevated fire weather concerns developing Monday as anomalously \nwarm afternoon temperatures combine with low afternoon humidity \nand breezy to locally windy conditions. This is most pronounced \nacross areas areas from the east slopes of the Rocky Mtn Front \neastward across areas adjacent to the central MT mountains where \nwind gusts in excess of 30-35 mph are most likely. While fuels are\nnot currently critical, these conditions will begin a trend \ntoward higher fire danger in the upcoming week. Hoenisch",
